## 11. Proposed `ml_lens_queries` Tool Operations

Based on the research, the tool should expose these operations:

```
ml_lens_queries.query_metric(
    namespace: str,           # e.g. "feature_observability.drift"
    metric_key: str,          # e.g. "ks_d"
    filter_by: Dict,          # e.g. {"feature_service": "fraud_v2"}
    time_range: Tuple[str,str], # ISO timestamps
    resolution: str = "FULL_RANGE",
    roll_up: str = "average"
) -> List[DataPoint]

ml_lens_queries.query_drift_status(
    feature_service: str,
    feature_name: Optional[str] = None,
    time_range: Tuple[str,str]
) -> DriftReport# Classified as DRIFT/WARNING/OK

ml_lens_queries.query_data_quality(
    namespace: str,
    table_or_feature: str,
    time_range: Tuple[str,str],
    metrics: List[str] = ["null_pct", "mean", "stddev", "p50", "p95"]
) -> QualityReport

ml_lens_queries.query_model_performance(
    namespace: str,
    model_id: Optional[str] = None,
    metrics: List[str] = ["NDCG@1", "MRR"],
    filter_by: Dict = {},
    time_range: Tuple[str,str]
) -> PerformanceReport

ml_lens_queries.discover_metrics(
    namespace_prefix: str,
    time_range: Tuple[str,str]
) -> List[MetricDescriptor]

ml_lens_queries.check_no_data(
    namespace: str,
    metric_key: str,
    since: str# ISO timestamp
) -> bool  # True if no data since that time
```

Each operation wraps the underlying `GET /api/v1/metrics/aggregated-analysis/{namespace.key}` endpoint (or equivalent SQL path via the SDK's `MlLensClient`), adding interpretation logic (threshold classification) and structured return types suitable for AI employee reasoning.[0m
