## 3. Feature Store Operations — Tecton/MLFS Lifecycle

### What it is
The feature lifecycle at Atlassian follows: **generated → written → hydrated → logged → joined-with-labels**. This covers both Tecton (being deprecated by Oct 2026) and MLFS (the replacement). [Tecton to MLFS Migration](https://home.atlassian.com/o/2346a038-3c8c-498b-a79b-e7847859868d/s/a436116f-02ce-4520-8fbb-7301462a1674/project/ATLAS-112694)

### How it works — Feature Lifecycle Stages

| Stage | Description | Tooling |
|-------|-------------|---------|
| **Generated** | Feature transformation defined as Python Feature View (batch/streaming/real-time) | `tecton-feature-definitions` repo; Python DSL |
| **Written** | Materialized to online/offline stores on schedule or stream trigger | Tecton materialization jobs; MLFS batch/stream FV |
| **Hydrated** | Feature values fetched for online serving (sub-40ms latency) | Tecton serving API; MLFS ERS-backed serving |
| **Logged** | Feature values captured at inference time for training data generation | Feature logging pipeline → offline store |
| **Joined-with-labels** | Historical features joined with outcome labels for training dataset creation | Point-in-time correct join; backfill support |

### Adding a New Feature (from enterprise docs)
1. Define feature in `tecton-feature-definitions` repo as Python Feature View
2. Register in MLFS for discoverability and governance
3. Add to feature service (e.g., `search_relevance_v2`) in Tecton
4. Add to featureset in config (index-level or Tecton features)
5. Backfill historical values for training data
6. Run feature logging to generate training data with new feature
7. Retrain model with new feature included
8. Evaluate offline and online to confirm value

[How does Featurization work? (Tecton & MLFS)](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120201ddfffba6aec4290997f86108f193ad4/pages/6947673097/How+does+Featurization+work+Tecton+MLFS)

### Feature Freshness Dimensions
| Feature type | Update frequency | Staleness impact |
|---|---|---|
| 7-day click rate | Hourly | Low |
| 30-day view count | Daily | Low |
| Author activity | Daily | Low |
| User-document affinity | Hourly | Medium |

---

## 4. Feature Store Data Quality Audit — Query Templates

### What it is
The **Feature DQ pipeline** (§6SOP-2step 4) provides continuous, automated quality monitoring for all Tecton Feature Views. It runs a two-stage process daily. [Feature DQ: Unified Feature Store Data Quality Pipeline](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6853284375/Feature+DQ+Unified+Feature+Store+Data+Quality+Pipeline)

### How it works — Two-Stage Pipeline

#### Stage 1 — Profile (Raw Metric Collection)
**Notebook:** `src/feature_dq/data_quality/dq_stage1_profile_metrics.ipynb`
**Entry function:** `profile_all()` in `dq_checks.py`
**Output table:** `Production.core_ml_data.coreml_feature_quality_profiles`

| Check Type | What is measured | Granularity |
|---|---|---|
| **Zero_Value_Check** (Coverage) | % of rows where feature column = 0 | Per feature column |
| **Volume_Check** (Freshness proxy) | Day-over-day % change in row count | Per Feature View |
| **Freshness_Check** | Age in hours of latest materialized partition | Per Feature View |

#### Stage 2 — Apply thresholds → SUCCESS/FAILURE

**Metrics table schema** (`coreml_feature_quality_audit_results`):

| Column | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `feature_view_name` | STRING | Tecton Feature View name |
| `feature_name` | STRING | Column name |
| `actual_value` | DOUBLE | Raw computed metric |
| `threshold` | DOUBLE | Per-FV threshold from `feature_views.yaml` |
| `dq_type` | STRING | `Zero_Value_Check` / `Volume_Check` / `Freshness_Check` |
| `tecton_env` | STRING | Tecton workspace (e.g. `prod-filtered`) |
| `time_range_begin` | STRING | Start of transformation window |
| `time_range_end` | STRING | End of transformation window |
| `snapshot_date` | STRING | Partition date |
| `run_date` | DATE | Audit date |

### Query Templates for Coverage/Freshness/Sparsity per Connector × Region × Tenant

```sql
-- COVERAGE: Zero-value percentage per feature × connector × region
SELECT 
  feature_view_name,
  feature_name,
  connector_type,     -- e.g., 'jira', 'confluence', 'bitbucket'
  region,             -- e.g., 'us-east-1', 'eu-west-1'
  tenant_id,
  actual_value AS zero_pct,
  threshold,CASE WHEN actual_value > threshold THEN 'FAIL' ELSE 'PASS' END AS status
FROM coreml_feature_quality_audit_results
WHERE dq_type = 'Zero_Value_Check'
  AND snapshot_date = CURRENT_DATE -1
ORDER BY zero_pct DESC;

-- FRESHNESS: Partition age per feature view × region
SELECT
  feature_view_name,
  region,
  actual_value AS age_hours,
  threshold AS max_age_hours,
  CASE WHEN actual_value > threshold THEN 'STALE' ELSE 'FRESH' END AS status
FROM coreml_feature_quality_audit_results
WHERE dq_type = 'Freshness_Check'
  AND snapshot_date = CURRENT_DATE - 1
ORDER BY age_hours DESC;

-- SPARSITY (VOLUME): Day-over-day volume drop per FV × tenant
SELECT
  feature_view_name,
  tenant_id,
  actual_value AS dod_pct_change,
  threshold,
  CASE WHEN ABS(actual_value) > threshold THEN 'ANOMALY' ELSE 'NORMAL' END AS status
FROM coreml_feature_quality_audit_results
WHERE dq_type = 'Volume_Check'
  AND snapshot_date = CURRENT_DATE - 1
  AND ABS(actual_value) > 20-- surface significant drops
ORDER BY ABS(dod_pct_change) DESC;
```

### Feature Freshness Calculation (from RFC)
```
Feature Freshness = Current Time - Latest Event Timestamp in Feature View
```
Materialization uses `fv.run_transformation(start_time, end_time)` and `materialization_status()` for freshness (no data scan needed).

[RFC: MLFS Monitoring - Feature View Freshness](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6035577692/RFC+MLFS+Monitoring+-+Feature+View+Freshness)

