## 3. Train-Serve Consistency Checks

### What It Is
Training-serving skew is identified as "the most critical challenge" in the Tecton to MLFS Migration Roadmap. It occurs when feature values used during training differ from those served at inference time due to:
- Different transformation logic between offline (batch) and online (streaming) paths
- Stale data in the online store
- Point-in-time correctness violations in training data generation
- Different data sources between training and serving

### How It Works at Atlassian

**MLFS Streaming Feature Validation Framework** (from [MLFS Streaming Features: Validation framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/7025246033/)):

Three equivalences must be established:

| Comparison | Description | Method |
|-----------|-------------|--------|
| Flink SFV online == Tecton SFV online | MLFS/Tecton parity for online serving | ML Lens shadow comparison: serving layer fires events for both primary (Tecton) and shadow (MLFS) paths |
| MLFS SFV offline == Tecton SFV offline | Parity for batch materialisation | Generic ML Studio workflow comparing offline store values |
| Online store == Offline store | Within-system consistency | Transitive validation (if both above pass) |

**ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection** ([Design doc](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349/Design+ML+Lens+Platform+Data+Drift+Detection)):
- Computes drift metrics every 5 minutes
- Metrics: PSI, KS statistic, basic stats (mean, stddev, min, max), quality (null%, zero%)
- Supports **training dataset as reference baseline** for detecting training-serving skew
- Alert configuration via SDK with opt-in model

**Concrete Checks the AI Employee Should Run:**
1. **Schema parity** — Types and nullability match between offline training features and online serving features
2. **Value parity** — Sample keys from both stores, compare mean values, distributions, missing value rates
3. **Freshness delta** — Ensure online store freshness is within SLA of offline store
4. **Point-in-time correctness** — Validate that `event_timestamp` in training data correctly prevents data leakage

### How It Applies to the Skill
The skill should include:
- A "train-serve consistency audit" workflow that the AI runs periodically
- Specific queries/checks for each type of skew
- Escalation thresholds (e.g., >5% mean value deviation → P1 alert)
- Integration with ML Lens APIs for automated comparison

### Source
- [MLFS Streaming Features: Validation framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/7025246033/MLFS+Streaming+Features+Validation+framework)
- [Design ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349/Design+ML+Lens+Platform+Data+Drift+Detection)
- [Tecton to MLFS Migration Roadmap](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6068686994/Tecton+to+MLFS+Migration+Roadmap)

