## 3. Confluence Page Create/Update for PIR Drafting

### 3.1 Create PIR Page (v2API — Recommended)

**What it is**: Confluence Cloud v2 API for page creation.

**Endpoint**: `POST /wiki/api/v2/pages`
**Auth**: Basic auth or OAuth 2.0
**Headers**: `Accept: application/json`, `Content-Type: application/json`

**Body**:
```json
{
  "spaceId": "123456",
  "status": "current",
  "title": "PIR: INC-123 — Service-X Degradation (2026-05-24)",
  "parentId": "789012",
  "body": {
    "representation": "storage",
    "value": "<h2>Summary</h2><p>...</p><h2>Timeline</h2><table>...</table>"
  }
}
```

**Response**: HTTP 200/201 with page `id`, `title`, `_links.webui`

**Supported representations**:
- `storage` — HTML-like Confluence storage format (recommended for structured PIRs)
- `wiki` — Wiki markup (legacy)
- `atlas_doc_format` — ADF (for complex formatting)

**How it applies**: The tool should create PIR pages using `storage` representation, which supports:
- Tables (for timeline, action items)
- Headers (for PIR sections)
- Macros (for Jira issue links, status panels)
- Structured macro for `<ac:structured-macro ac:name="jira">` to embed issue links

**Source**: [Confluence API Endpoint Reference](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/migtool/pages/6749021590), [Cloud-native Process for Automating Confluence Space and Home Page Creation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020438c0e8ac5394e0d99bb004d7cdfed39/pages/6333275457)

---

### 3.2 Update PIR Page

**What it is**: Updating an existing Confluence page requires incrementing the version number.

**Endpoint**: `PUT /wiki/api/v2/pages/{pageId}`
**Body**:
```json
{
  "id": "123456",
  "status": "current",
  "title": "PIR: INC-123 — Service-X Degradation (2026-05-24)",
  "body": {
    "representation": "storage",
    "value": "<p>Updated content</p>"
  },
  "version": {
    "number": 2,
    "message": "AI PIR draft v2: added timeline from Slack thread"
  }
}
```

**Critical Constraint**: PUT replaces the ENTIRE body. To update a section:
1. GET current page body (`GET /wiki/api/v2/pages/{pageId}?body-format=storage`)
2. Parse/modify the relevant section
3. PUT the full modified body with incremented version number

**How it applies**: The `incident_pir_drafter` will:
1. Create initial PIR skeleton on incident resolution
2. Update the page as more data becomes available (Loom transcripts, Slack thread history)
3. Each update must fetch current version, increment, and PUT full body

**Source**: [Python Update Page Body API request](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DABA/pages/1399245781), [Confluence Cloud - Editor - Band 3.5](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CTH/pages/4481253509)

---

### 3.3 PIR Template Structure (Atlassian Standard)

**What it is**: The standard PIR structure used across Atlassian:

```html
<h2>Summary</h2>
<ac:structured-macro ac:name="details">
  <ac:rich-text-body>
    <table><tbody>
      <tr><th>Incident</th><td>{HOT-key}</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Severity</th><td>{Sev N}</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Impact Duration</th><td>{HH:MM}</td></tr>
      <tr><th>TTD</th><td>{MM min}</td></tr>
      <tr><th>TTR</th><td>{HH:MM}</td></tr>
      <tr><th>PIR Owner</th><td>{name}</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </ac:rich-text-body>
</ac:structured-macro>

<h2>Incident Summary</h2>
<p>{Debrief: key events, decisions, detection, customer impact, mitigation}</p>

<h2>Lead-Up</h2>
<p>{Circumstances that led to incident, prior changes}</p>

<h2>Fault</h2>
<p>{What broke and how. Attach screenshots/graphs}</p>

<h2>Impact</h2>
<p>{Customer impact, affected products/services, blast radius, support tickets}</p>

<h2>Detection</h2>
<p>{Who detected, how, any delays}</p>

<h2>Response</h2>
<p>{Who responded, when, how, any delays}</p>

<h2>Recovery</h2>
<p>{How/when service restored}</p>

<h2>Timeline</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Timestamp (UTC)</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td>{ts}</td><td>{event description}</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<h2>Five Whys</h2>
<p>{Iterative "Why" from impact to root cause}</p>

<h2>Root Cause</h2>
<p>{The thing that needs to change}</p>

<h2>Lessons Learned</h2>
<p>{Systemic changes to prevent recurrence}</p>

<h2>Actions</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Owner</th><th>Jira Ticket</th><th>Due</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td>{action}</td><td>{owner}</td><td>{PROJ-123}</td><td>{date}</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<h2>Risks</h2>
<p>{Ongoing risks exposed}</p>

<h2>Where We Got Lucky</h2>
<p>{External factors that helped}</p>
```

**PIR Actions Guidance** (from template):
1. **Deployment and Testing**: Rollback strategies, peer review
2. **Incident Timelines**: TTD < 15m, TTR < 2h improvements
3. **Recovery and Restoration**: Reduce recovery times
4. **Monitoring and Alerting**: Detection improvements
5. **Prevention**: Recurrence prevention
6. **Impact Quantification**: SLO/SLA deviation correction
7. **Playbook Updates**: Runbook improvements
8. **Incident Management**: Engagement speed, severity accuracy

**How it applies**: The tool generates this entire structure automatically by:
- Reading incident fields from JSM (severity, affected services, responders)
- Reading Slack thread (for timeline, decisions, actions)
- Reading any linked Loom transcripts
- Auto-populating TTD/TTR/TTC calculations
- Leaving🟡 human-review sections (Five Whys, Root Cause, Actions) partially filled with AI suggestions

**Source**: [Incident Response Plan — PIR Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/gir/pages/1540798526), [TEMPLATE: PIR (for automation only)](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/IMPIR/pages/3543870301), [Post-Incident Review Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~617068837/pages/6179670614)

