# Research Report: SRE Incident-Management Frameworks for ml-incident-response SKILL.md

## 1. Incident Timing Metrics Framework (TTD / TTH / TTR / TTC)

### 1.1 Definitions (Atlassian Standard)

| Metric | Definition | Formula |
|--------|-----------|---------|
| **TTD** (Time to Detect) | Time from when users/systems first experience impact until the issue is detected (manually or automated) | `Impact Detection Time − Impact Start Time` |
| **TTH** (Time to HOT) | Time from impact start until the HOT ticket reaches its current severity level | `HOT Creation Time − Impact Start Time` |
| **TTE** (Time to Engage) | Time until the correct root-cause-owning team is engaged | `Engagement Time − Impact Start Time` |
| **TTC** (Time to Communicate) | Elapsed time from HOT creation until first external communication if customers affected | `First External Comms − HOT Creation Time` |
| **TTR** (Time to Resolve/Mitigate) | Time from impact start until service is restored to normal | `Resolution Time − Impact Start Time` |

**Source**: [Core Engineering Monthly Operations Review](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CoreEngineering/pages/4159315444), [TEMPLATE: PIR (for automation only)](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/IMPIR/pages/3543870301), [RFC: How to measure TTD for Major Incidents](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/Spork/pages/4440107932)

### 1.2 Atlassian SLO Targets

| Severity | TTD Target | TTR Target | First Response SLO | Communication Cadence |
|----------|-----------|-----------|-------------------|----------------------|
| **Sev0** | ≤10 min | ≤60 min (proposed), ≤240 min (current) | 15 min | Every 15 min |
| **Sev1** | ≤15 min | ≤90 min (proposed), ≤240 min (current) | 15 min | Every 15 min |
| **Sev2** | ≤30 min | ≤120 min (proposed), ≤240 min (current) | 15 min | Every 15–30 min |
| **Sev3** | ≤2 business hours | ≤1 business day | 4 business hours | Every 30–60 min |

**Source**: [Policy - Incident Management](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/PMP/pages/4116450764), [Incident Severity TTD and TTR Mapping Proposal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202092bf5a008d0044d7b6c218d07fd921ad/pages/4214167994), [Reliability Incident Response Atlas Goal](https://home.atlassian.com/o/2346a038-3c8c-498b-a79b-e7847859868d/s/a436116f-02ce-4520-8fbb-7301462a1674/goal/ATLAS-59190)

### 1.3 TTR Breakdown — "Happy Path" Baseline

From [TTR Breakdown Analysis](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~522087587/pages/4060422688):

| Phase | Target Time |
|-------|-------------|
| TTD (Time to Detect) | 30 min |
| Hot Creation After Detection | 10 min |
| MIM Page Acknowledge | 5 min |
| MIM HOT Acknowledge | 5 min |
| MIM First Page (to owning team) | 10 min |
| Mitigation Identification (after first page) | 20 min |
| Time between Identification & Mitigation Attempt | 5 min |
| Time to Implement Mitigation | 30 min |
| Time to Validate Fix | 5 min |
| **Total TTR** | **~120 min** |

### 1.4 How This Applies to ml-incident-response SKILL.md

- The skill should encode **TTD < 15 min** and **TTR < 2 hr** as hard targets for ML system incidents (matching the role doc §6SOP-3)
- The skill workflow should track each phase (detect → classify → engage → mitigate → validate) with timestamps
- 30-min status cadence should be enforced by the AI agent posting to Slack threads
- The AI should calculate and report these metrics in the PIR draft automatically
- Key insight: **100% of incidents resolved within 2h were detected within 30 minutes** — so the skill must prioritize fast detection above all else

---

## 2. Severity Classification Matrix

### 2.1 Atlassian Standard Severity Levels

| Severity | Type | Definition | Response Mode |
|----------|------|-----------|---------------|
| **Sev0** | Crisis | Substantial impact to Atlassian brand; major re-prioritization of company initiatives | 24/7, all-hands, CMOC + SuperMIM paged, ELT Slack room |
| **Sev1** | Critical/Major | Business impact exceeds any non-emergency activity; requires executive/org resources beyond engineering | 24/7, MIM + CMOC paged, ELT monitoring |
| **Sev2** | Major (Significant) | Business impact exceeds non-emergency activity; core functionality unavailable or degraded with customer impact | 24/7, MIM paged |
| **Sev3** | Minor | Low impact; top priority during business hours only; NOT a service request | Business hours, team handles |

**Source**: [Understanding Incident Severity](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/IMPIR/pages/5142053110), [Standard - Incident Severity](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/PMP/pages/3022620379)

### 2.2 Service Tier × Impact Matrix

From [Standard - Incident Severity](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/PMP/pages/3022620379):

**Total Service Outage:**

| Duration | Tier3 (Lowest) | Tier 2 | Tier 1 | Tier 0 (Highest) |
|----------|----------------|--------|--------|-------------------|
| < 5 min | N/A | N/A | Sev3 | Sev2 |
| 5–60 min | N/A | Sev3 | Sev2 | Sev1 |
| > 60 min | Sev3 | Sev2 | Sev1 | Sev1 |

**Degraded Service:**

| Impact Type | Tier 3 | Tier 2 | Tier 1 | Tier 0 |
|-------------|--------|--------|--------|--------|
| Core Functionality Broken | N/A | Sev3 | Sev2 | Sev1 |
| Non-Core Broken | N/A | N/A | Sev3 | Sev2 |
| Significant Perf Degradation | N/A | Sev3 | Sev2 | Sev1 |
| Noticeable Perf Degradation | N/A | N/A | Sev3 | Sev2 |

**Data Loss:**

| Type | Any Tier |
|------|----------|
| Customer data within RPO | Sev2 |
| Customer data exceeding RPO,<3% customers | Sev1 |
| Customer data exceeding RPO, ≥3% customers | Sev0 |

### 2.3 Factors for Severity Classification

From [Policy - Incident Management](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/PMP/pages/4116450764):

1. **Impact** — Service completely down, degraded, high latency, intermittent errors
2. **Extent** — Number of internal/external customers affected
3. **Capability** — Core vs. non-core functionality
4. **Duration of impact** — As incidents go on, severity increases; factor in responder availability
5. **Customer vs. internal** — Customer-facing = higher business impact
6. **Data loss** — Always increases severity
7. **Security breach/risk** — Usually Sev0 if active compromise

### 2.4 ML-System-Specific Severity Classification (To Be Encoded in Skill)

Based on the role doc §6 SOP-3 and general framework, for the responsible-ai-api and ML-Engine services:

| Severity | ML-Specific Criteria |
|----------|---------------------|
| **Sev1** | Model serving complete outage (all inference requests failing); data poisoning detected; unauthorized model deployment |
| **Sev2** | >20% error rate on inference; drift exceeding safety thresholds with bias impact; circuit breaker open with customer-visible degradation; moderation bypass |
| **Sev3** | Elevated latency (>2x P99); drift detected but within acceptable bounds; single-tenant rate limiting triggered; non-critical model degradation |

### 2.5 How This Applies to ml-incident-response SKILL.md

- The skill should implement a **cross-correlation** step: check ML metrics (error rate, latency, drift score) against the service-tier matrix to auto-classify severity
- The AI agent must follow "when in doubt, escalate" principle per Atlassian policy
- Severity can change mid-incident — the skill must support severity upgrades/downgrades with recorded justification
- For ML systems specifically, the skill should cross-reference the circuit breaker state (20%/60s sliding window) to inform severity

---

## 3. PIR (Post-Incident Review) Templates and Process

### 3.1 PIR Lifecycle

From [Incident Response Plan](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/gir/pages/1540798526) and [Post-Incident Reviews (PIRs)](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020569da089a21d46c89d014c3c8a40039d/pages/6272935146):

| Severity | PIR Required? | Time to Draft SLA | Time to Approval SLA |
|----------|--------------|-------------------|---------------------|
| Sev0 | ✅ Mandatory (Full) | 30 business days | 14 business days |
| Sev1 | ✅ Mandatory (Full) | 30 business days | 14 business days |
| Sev2 | Optional (Full or PIR-X) | 30 business days | 14 business days |
| Sev3 | Optional (PIR-X default) | — | — |

**PIR-X** = Express/lightweight PIR with fewer fields and optional meetings.

### 3.2 PIR Data Model — Two Artifacts

1. **OpsJ PIR Ticket (SSoT)**: Structured data — TTD, TTR, severity, root-cause categories, linked Jira action items, HOT links, parent/child PIR relationships, status lifecycle (draft → review → approved)
2. **Confluence PIR Document (Narrative)**: Story, timeline, impact analysis, causes/contributing factors, learnings, recommended actions

### 3.3 Atlassian Standard PIR Template (Confluence)

From multiple PIR templates observed across the org ([PIR Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/loomhq/pages/4475258324), [Incident Response Program - PIR Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~566781925/pages/968427754)):

```
## Incident Overview
- Investigators/Authors
- Incident responders  
- Hot Ticket
- PIR Ticket

### Narrative
[Full story of what happened]

### Impact
- Customer/user impact and business risk
- Quantified: affected users, duration, throughput delta, error rates
- SLO(s) breached

### Trigger
[What initiated the chain of events]

### Timeline
| Date/Time (UTC) | Event | Notes |
| Impact start|       |       |
| Detection|       |       |
| HOT created|       |       |
| Mitigation      |       |       |
| Resolution      |       |       |

## Resolution

### Mitigators
[What stopped the bleeding]

### Root Cause — Conditions / Failed Barriers / Events
[Causal tree or5Whys analysis]

### Actions
**High priority (8weeks SLA)**
**Medium priority (14 weeks SLA)**  
**Low priority (20 weeks SLA)**
**Improvement (No SLA)**

## Learnings
### Lessons
### Process difficulties
### Risks
```

### 3.4 Five Whys Framework

From [Post-incident reviews (PIRs)](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020276fbb3d715d487684e842ea00dad0cf/pages/5298729532) and [PIR Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/loomhq/pages/4475258324):

Structured questions (not generic "5 whys"):
1. **Why did we not catch the problem before it happened?**
2. **Why did it take us [duration] to notice the problem after it impacted customers?**
3. **Why did it take us [duration] to raise a HOT?**
4. **Why did it take [duration] to resolve the HOT?**
5. **Why did resolving the customer data problem take [duration]?**

Each "why" can branch into multiple causal threads. Stop each branch when reaching:
- A fundamental cause that can be addressed
- Insufficient data (create action to gather more)
- Issue not resolvable organizationally (consider what's within power)

### 3.5 PIR Action Types and SLAs

From [Incident Response Program - PIR Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~566781925/pages/968427754):

| Action Type | SLO | Description |
|-------------|-----|-------------|
| PIR - HIGH PRIORITY ACTION | 56 days (8 weeks) | Directly addresses root cause |
| PIR - MEDIUM PRIORITY ACTION | 98 days (14 weeks) | Addresses contributing causes |
| PIR - LOW PRIORITY ACTION | 140 days (20 weeks) | Low risk, low cost improvement |
| IMPROVEMENT ACTION | Not tracked by RPG | Low risk, high cost strategic work |
| PIR - NEGOTIATED ACTION | Negotiated with owning team | Edge cases |

### 3.6 PIR Approver Checklist

From [PIR Template - Section for PIR Approver Checklist](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/cadp1/pages/4350282215):

- [ ] **Timely Communications**: Internal/external comms sent per20-min initial SLA
- [ ] **Incident dates verified**: Impact Start< Detection< Hot Time < Resolution
- [ ] **SLOs linked**: Affected SLOs linked to PIR ticket
- [ ] **Actions address "Preventable by"**: Must address root cause category
- [ ] **Actions address "Recovery by"**: Especially where service didn't self-heal
- [ ] **High priority TTD Actions**: Any manually detected or TTD > 30 min needs high-priority action
- [ ] **TTR Actions**: Any TTR > 2hr needs resolution actions (rollback, scale up, restart, runbook gaps)

### 3.7 PIR Actions Guidance (Standard Template)

From multiple PIR documents, actions should address:

1. **Deployment and Testing** — peer review, pre-checkin, post-deployment validations, rollback strategies
2. **Incident Timelines** — TTD < 15 min, TTR < 2 hr targets
3. **Recovery and Restoration** — reduce recovery times
4. **Monitoring and Alerting** — detect issues promptly
5. **Prevention** — ensure similar incidents don't recur
6. **Impact Quantification** — fix SLO/SLA measurement gaps
7. **Playbook Updates** — revise runbook based on this incident
8. **Incident Management** — engagement speed, severity accuracy, handoff minimization

### 3.8 How This Applies to ml-incident-response SKILL.md

The AI agent should:
- **Auto-draft the PIR Confluence page** using the standard template with ML-specific additions (drift stats, model version, feature importance changes, circuit breaker states)
- **Auto-populate the timeline** from Slack thread timestamps + alert timestamps
- **Calculate TTD/TTR metrics** and compare against SLO targets
- **Generate "Five Whys" prompts** pre-filled with incident data (durations, detection method)
- **Suggest categorized action items** based on whether the gap was in detection (monitoring), prevention (testing/gating), or recovery (rollback/circuit-breaker)
- **Link affected SLOs** from the IaC-defined per-endpoint SLOs
- **PIR must be completed within 5 business days** per role doc §6 SOP-3

