## 8. Autonomy Tiering for Incident Response

### 8.1 Three-Tier Autonomy Model (From Role Doc §10.5)

| Tier | Color | AI Action | Human Interaction |
|------|-------|-----------|-------------------|
| 🟢 Green | Execute autonomously | Notification only (post-hoc) |
| 🟡 Yellow | Draft + propose | Human must confirm before execution |
| 🔴 Red | Escalate immediately | Human must drive; AI assists only |

### 8.2 Mapping to Incident Response Actions

| Action | Autonomy Tier | Rationale |
|--------|--------------|-----------|
| Detect anomaly / fire alert | 🟢 | Read-only, no harm potential |
| Classify severity (initial) | 🟢 | Algorithm-driven, can be corrected |
| Post Slack status update (internal) | 🟢 | Informational, no customer impact |
| Create HOT ticket | 🟡 | Creates organizational obligation |
| Escalate to MIM/CMOC | 🟡 | Pages humans out of hours |
| Execute circuit breaker toggle | 🟡 | Service behavior change |
| Post external status page update | 🔴 | Customer-facing brand impact |
| Model rollback in production | 🟡 | Service behavior change, reversible |
| Draft PIR |🟢 | Document creation, requires approval |
| Close incident | 🟡 | Ends tracking/escalation |

### 8.3 How This Applies to ml-incident-response SKILL.md

- Each step in the workflow must be annotated with its autonomy tier
- 🟢 actions can be executed without interruption
- 🟡 actions must present a confirmation prompt and wait for human response (or timeout → escalate)
- 🔴 actions should gather context and present to human with recommendations but never execute autonomously

