─── Response ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Key findings:


 1 Multi-seed (mean±std): Require ≥3 seeds (5 for ship, 10 for headline
   launches), report SE not just std, prefer paired bootstrap (BCa, B=10k)
   over normal-approx CIs, and use paired tests (Wilcoxon / ttest_rel) with
   same-seed pairing for deltas. Track Bouthillier-taxonomy variance sources
   (init, data order, augmentation, hardware non-determinism).
 2 Splits: Auto-select via decision tree — StratifiedGroupKFold is the right
   default for ConvoAI/LLM multi-turn
   (group=conversation_id/user_id/prompt_id), TimeSeriesSplit with gap≥horizon
   for temporal, purged k-fold + embargo (López de Prado) when labels overlap.
   The runner must warn when a group_col is present but plain KFold is chosen.
 3 Calibration: Always compute ECE (15 adaptive/equal-mass bins), Brier, NLL,
   and a reliability diagram. Use bootstrap CIs paired across models, Wilson
   per-bin. Recalibrate via temperature scaling (LLM default) or isotonic;
   never report calibration on the same split used to fit it.
 4 Fairness slicing: Beyond protected attributes, require operational slices
   (tenant, locale, surface, query-length, time-of-day). Compute
   selection-rate, equalized-odds, calibration-parity per slice with BH-FDR 
   q=0.10 across slices. Report worst-slice / CVaR_0.10 as the headline
   number. Tooling: fairlearn.MetricFrame.
 5 Failure taxonomy: Mandatory for any ship recommendation. Protocol:
   stratified-sample errors → open-code → 5–12 MECE categories → rubric with
   examples → ≥2 raters → Cohen's/Fleiss' κ ≥ 0.6 gate. LLM-as-judge OK only
   after human validation of κ; mitigate position/length/self-preference bias.
 6 Implementation contract: Fail-closed validators in the runners (refuse to
   render if seeds<3, taxonomy missing for "ship", paired-comparison metadata
   missing, pre-registered practical-significance threshold absent). Single
   eval_artifact.json schema feeds all downstream generators
   (experiment_report_generator, model_card_generator,
   deployment_manifest_generator, weekly_experiment_digest) so rigor lives in
   the runner and generators only render layout.
 7 Library inventory provided mapping each concern (bootstrap, paired tests,
   FDR, splits, calibration, slicing, rater-agreement, behavioral testing) to
   canonical Python primitives — enables the new generators to be thin
   templating layers over scipy, sklearn, statsmodels, fairlearn, netcal,
   iterstrat, checklist.
 8 Open questions flagged for synthesis: existing rovo-eval/aix-eval coverage
   of these primitives, ML-Studio slice-metadata availability for fairness
   joins, mcp__socrates__ cost-key alignment with experiment_id, and netcal
   vs. torchmetrics org-wide standardization.
