## 5. Responsible-AI-API Production Patterns Checklist

### What Internal Docs Cover (Synthesized)
- Two-Stage Prompt Moderator architecture exists (Gemma4Stage 1 → GPT-OSS 20B Stage 2)
- Feature gates: `rai_api_enable_cascade_moderation`, `rai_api_cascade_skip_stage2` (kill-switch)
- Per-tenant token bucket rate limiting (Redis-backed, Hofund/Heimdall)
- Fail-open behavior on Redis failure with circuit breaker
- Canary analysis via Spinnaker with Mann-Whitney U test

### Distilled Reusable Checklist for Deployment Skill

#### 5.1 Two-Stage Moderation Deployment Checklist
```
PRE-DEPLOYMENT:
□ Stage 1 model (fast classifier) registered in Tarot v3
□ Stage 2 model (high-precision) registered in Tarot v3
□ Feature gates configured:
  □ Master rollout gate (percentage per tenant)
  □ Stage 1 variant selector
  □ Stage 2 skip kill-switch (for latency incidents)
□ Metrics instrumented:
  □ stage1.latency_ms, stage2.latency_ms
  □ stage1.blocking_rate, e2e.blocking_rate
  □ stage2.overturn_rate, stage2.precision
□ SLA targets documented: P50≤ 150ms (S1), <0.35% e2e block rate

DEPLOYMENT:
□ Deploy to staging, run shadow traffic for 24h
□ Verify Stage 1 blocking rate ~2% (within ±0.5%)
□ Verify Stage 2 overturn rate matches offline eval
□ Progressive rollout: 1% → 10% → 50% → 100% of tenants
□ At each step: wait 15min, verify SLO metrics

POST-DEPLOYMENT:
□ Monitor for 72h post-100%
□ Verify no tenant-specific anomalies in blocking rate
□ Document: actual vs. expected metrics, any deviations
```

#### 5.2 Fail-Open Kill-Switch Pattern
```
IMPLEMENTATION CHECKLIST:
□ Kill-switch feature gate defined (instant toggle, no deploy needed)
□ Fail-open behavior:
  □ On moderation timeout → ALLOW with metric tag `reason=timeout`
  □ On moderation error → ALLOW with metric tag `reason=error`
  □ On kill-switch active → ALLOW with metric tag `reason=killswitch`
□ Circuit breaker: opens after 5 consecutive failures within 30s
□ Metrics:
  □ fail_open.count (tagged by reason)
  □ fail_open.duration_seconds (how long in fail-open state)
□ Alert: if fail_open.count > 100/min → page oncall
□ Runbook: link to incident response for sustained fail-open

VALIDATION:
□ Chaos test: force moderation timeout, verify fail-open activates
□ Chaos test: toggle kill-switch, verify immediate fail-open
□ Verify: fail-open does NOT cascade to other tenants
□ Verify: metrics correctly attributed
```

#### 5.3 Per-Tenant Token Bucket Checklist
```
CONFIGURATION:
□ Bucket size per tenant defined (e.g., 800 requests burst)
□ Refill rate defined (e.g., 300 tokens/second)
□ Redis-backed counters for cross-pod accuracy
□ Tenant isolation: separate bucket per tenant per endpoint
□ Override capability for specific tenants (elevated limits)

SAFETY:
□ Fail-open on Redis failure (rate limiting disabled, metric emitted)
□ Feature flag for instant rollback to no-rate-limiting
□ Circuit breaker on Redis: opens after N consecutive failures
□ Key TTL set to prevent unbounded memory growth

OBSERVABILITY:
□ near_limit_count metric (>80% consumed)
□ over_limit_count metric (rejected requests)
□ redis_error_count metric
□ Per-tenant dashboard showing consumption patterns

ROLLOUT:
□ Shadow mode first: log decisions without enforcement
□ Gradual enforcement: 5% → 50% → 100% of traffic
□ Monitor for false positives during shadow mode
□ Validate: no single tenant affected disproportionately
```

#### 5.4 Canary Auto-Rollback Checklist
```
PRE-DEPLOYMENT:
□ Canary config YAML in repository (canary-config.yml)
□ Health signals defined:
  □ Availability SLO (from TOME)
  □ API Reliability SLO
  □ 5xx error rate (spinnaker-default-5xx)
  □ Custom service-specific metrics
□ Progressive states defined: 10% → 25% → 50% → 100%
□ Analysis window: 15min per state
□ Rollback behavior: automatic (or manual judgment with20min timeout)

DEPLOYMENT:
□ Canary deploys to new stack alongside baseline
□ At each progressive step:
  □ Mann-Whitney U test compares canary vs. baseline metrics
  □ Pass = proceed to next step
  □ Fail = automatic rollback (or manual judgment)
□ Monitor: latency, error rate, CPU/memory, custom metrics

POST-DEPLOYMENT:
□ After100%: keep baseline stack for 1h (instant rollback capability)
□ Verify: no anomalies in first hour at full traffic
□ Announce completion in #deployments channel
□ Remove baseline stack only after soak period
```
- **Source**: Synthesized from Atlassian Spinnaker Safe Release, Shepherd progressive rollouts, Netflix Kayenta, Google SRE workbook Chapter 17(Canarying Releases)

