## Finding 7: ORBIT Signal Dimensions & Feature Set

### What it is
ORBIT normalizes activity signals into a unified feature set used for ranking entities in the Inner/Near/Outer layers.

### How it works
From [ORBIT Platform Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AICT/pages/7049333779) and [ORBIT - MVP](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AICT/pages/6342833719):

**Five normalized signal dimensions:**
1. **Recency decay** — How recently the user/agent interacted with the entity
2. **Frequency** — How often interactions occur
3. **Ownership strength** — How strong the ownership/responsibility signal is
4. **Collaboration intensity** — How many team members are co-active on this entity
5. **Urgency** — Time-sensitivity signals (deadlines, SLO burns, incident severity)

**Three orbit layers:**
- **Inner** — Core working set; directly owned/assigned; active in last 3 days
- **Near** — Adjacent work; collaborated on; reviewed recently; blocking/blocked-by Inner items
- **Outer** — Peripheral awareness; same project/team; trending; historically important

**Four ranking dimensions (from ORBIT Platform Strategy):**
1. **Who** — People/teams most relevant
2. **What** — Work items, documents, code most relevant
3. **Where** — Projects, spaces, repos most relevant
4. **How** — Methods, tools, patterns most relevant

**Platform metrics:**
| Metric | Target |
|--------|--------|
| Context pack serve latency (p99) | <100ms |
| Ranking freshness (p95) | <24h |
| Signal ingestion error rate | <0.1% |
| Pipeline uptime | ≥99.9% |
| Daily ranking throughput | ≥200M pairs/day |

### How it applies to `symphony_orbit_enrichment`
When the tool writes enrichment signals, it must:
1. Map the outcome type to the correct signal dimensions (e.g., incident resolution → urgency↓ + recency_decay_reset + collaboration_intensity↑)
2. Target the correct entity ARIs
3. Respect the batch pipeline cadence (currently<24h freshness SLO)
4. Include reason codes that will be surfaced to future context pack consumers

### Source
- [ORBIT Platform Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AICT/pages/7049333779)
- [ORBIT - MVP](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AICT/pages/6342833719)

---

## Finding 8: SRE-Specific ORBIT Bootstrap Context

### What it is
When Symphony spawns a Digital SRE agent, ORBIT loads a context pack specifically scoped to SRE work.

### How it works
From [Atlassian Symphony](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6933824809):

**SRE context pack contents:**
- Service architecture (dependency graph, topology)
- Incident history (past HOTs, postmortems, Causal Graphs, Permanent Remediation PRs)
- SLOs (thresholds, burn rates, error budgets)
- Open issues (current work items, blockers)
- Deployment cadence (release history, canary patterns)
- Alert definitions (detectors, thresholds, known noisy alerts)
- Runbooks (human-authored playbooks, machine-executable policies)

**MLE context pack contents:**
- Model lineage
- Experiment history
- Data quality signals

### How it applies to `symphony_orbit_enrichment`
The enrichment tool must support writing outcomes that update each of these SRE context pack components:
- `enrich_incident_history(service_ari, incident_data)` → Adds resolved incident to the pack
- `enrich_slo_state(service_ari, slo_data)` → Updates current SLO burn/budget
- `enrich_deployment_outcome(service_ari, deploy_data)` → Records canary/rollout outcome
- `enrich_runbook(service_ari, runbook_data)` → Updates runbook freshness and content
- `enrich_alert_snr(service_ari, alert_data)` → Updates signal-to-noise classification

---

## Finding 9: Policy-as-Code & Governance Rotation

### What it is
In the agentic operations model, policies are explicit, version-controlled constraints that determine autonomous agent behavior. They live in the repo alongside the Spec.

### How it works
From [Agentic Operations in the New SDLC](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6885869718):

**Policy examples:**
| Policy Type | Example | Agent Behavior |
|-------------|---------|----------------|
| SLO Contract | All payment services must have99.99% availability, P99 ≤ 100ms | Auto-configures monitoring, error budgets, alert thresholds |
| Rollout Gate | Roll back if P95 latency increases >10% or error rate >0.5% in canary | Executes rollback autonomously; notifies SRE |
| Cost Guardrail | Never auto-scale above3× baseline without human approval | Caps scaling; pages SRE at ceiling |
| Escalation Boundary | Page human when fix carries >$10k cost or affects >1% sessions | Defines threshold where autonomous action stops |

**What can't be expressed as policy (always🔴 Escalate):**
- Strategic ambiguity: two fixes both carry negative business impact
- Context the agent doesn't have: regulatory exposure, customer relationship sensitivity, upcoming announcements

### How it applies to `symphony_orbit_enrichment`
The tool should:
1. Check the agent's policy scope before any enrichment write
2. Include policy version in the enrichment audit trail
3. Support a `propose_policy_update` operation when the agent identifies a gap (🟡 tier)
4. Never permit enrichment that would modify policy without human approval (🔴 tier)

---

## Finding 10: Hybrid Architecture — Offline + Real-time Signal Merge

### What it is
ORBIT uses a hybrid architecture combining offline long-term signals (1d–90d) with real-time short-term signals (<1d) from TwG Context APIs.

### How it works
From [Orbit x TwG Sync: April](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AICT/pages/6890691540):

```
┌────────────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────────┐
│ Orbit (Offline)│     │ TwG (Real-time)          │
│ TwG Relationship Table│     │ TwG Context APIs         │
│ → ML Studio Pipeline    │     │ → Recent Activities│
│ → Ranked Scores in MLFS │     │   (last 7 days)          │
└───────────┬────────────┘     └───────────┬─────────────┘
            │                               │
            └───────────┬───────────────────┘
                        │
                  Parallel Merge        │
                        ▼
              Rovo Chat / PQS / QuickFind
```

| Layer | Strengths | Limitations |
|-------|-----------|-------------|
| Orbit (offline) | Days-to-months signals; fast MLFS retrieval; heavy ML ranking | Upfront compute; static until next pipeline run (1d gap) |
| TwG (real-time) | Fresh data (5min–hours); low integration overhead | Slow for long spans; simple ML only |
| Hybrid (parallel) | Long-term relevance + real-time recency | Requires merge logic; two data paths |

### How it applies to `symphony_orbit_enrichment`
The enrichment tool operates at the boundary between real-time and batch:
- **Urgent enrichments** (incident resolution, SLO breach) should write to the real-time TwG activity layer for immediate visibility
- **Non-urgent enrichments** (experiment results, runbook updates) can write to the batch pipeline for next-cycle incorporation
- The tool should expose a `priority` parameter: `immediate` vs. `batch`

